### 1. Ensure the user enters valid data:
Make sure the user enters a valid integer before attempting to read it into an `int`. If they enter something other than an integer, it will throw an exception.

### 2. Handle the exception:
Wrap your code inside a try-catch block to catch and handle the `InputMismatchException`.

Here’s how you could do it:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("Enter a number:");

        int number;

        try {
            number = scanner.nextInt();
            System.out.println("You entered: " + number);
        } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter a valid integer.");
        }
    }
}
```

